SUMMARY:Princeton Bioethics Exchange
LOCATION:Briger Hall\, B431 Conference Room
DESCRIPTION:Princeton biophysicist Robert Austin's recent work has examin
 ed how bacteria can be driven to evolve defenses against viruses that in
 fect them. But what if we flip that approach and engineer viruses that o
 vercome bacterial defenses? Prof. Austin will lead a conversation about 
 what these experiments could teach us and whether they should be pursued
 . Led and co-moderated by Penelope Georges of the Center on Science and 
 Technology. Interested postdocs and grad students should contact Ryan Ma
 cElroy (rm5962@princeton.edu) for more details. Lunch will be provided. 
 12:00-1:30 PM\nAudience: Faculty & Academic Professionals\nhttps://www.p
 rinceton.edu/events/2026/princeton-bioethics-exchange
URL:https://www.princeton.edu/events/2026/princeton-bioethics-exchange
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
END:VCALENDAR
