DTEND:20260923T213000Z
SUMMARY:From Rulers’ Dwellings to Temples\, Thirty Years On | PITHOS (P
 rinceton-Ioannina-Thessaly On-Site Seminars) Graduate Exchange Program L
 ecture
LOCATION:Art Museum\, 134 Small Auditorium
DESCRIPTION:In my lecture\, I will return to an earlier thesis of mine in
  order to reassess its validity. The main idea posited that in the Early
  Iron Age\, at least until the mid 8th century B.C.\, certain functions 
 later associated with urban temples were originally fulfilled by the dwe
 llings of the “rulers” or\, more generally\, by the residences of lo
 cal elites. The primary role of these edifices was to host large-scale f
 easts\, some of which likely had a ritual character. This situation grad
 ually changed as socio-political conditions transformed\, eventually lea
 ding to the emergence of the polis and the subsequent isonomia among cit
 izens. The review of the evidence\, derived from excavations conducted o
 ver the past decades as well as new evaluations of older data\, does not
  appear to contradict this premise.\nAudience: Open to the Public\, Facu
 lty & Academic Professionals\, Staff\, Students\nhttps://www.princeton.e
 du/events/2026/rulers-dwellings-temples-thirty-years-pithos-princeton-io
 annina-thessaly-site-seminars
URL:https://www.princeton.edu/events/2026/rulers-dwellings-temples-thirty
 -years-pithos-princeton-ioannina-thessaly-site-seminars
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
END:VCALENDAR
