UID:4213f71439ab4440a771d404e9c0f8bd
DTSTAMP:20260920T025617Z
DTSTART:20260926T000000Z
DTEND:20260926T010000Z
SUMMARY:Seuls en Scène 2026 presents Vivantes by Aurélie Charon
LOCATION:Wallace Theater\, Lewis Arts Complex
DESCRIPTION:The team of Radio Live returns to Princeton for the fourth ti
 me to present a new installment of their fascinating series: Vivantes. V
 ivantes is a unique stage experience that transcends the boundaries betw
 een radio and theater. Conceived by Aurélie Charon and Amélie Bonnin\,
  this hybrid work brings to life the voices of two remarkable young peop
 le: Oksana Leuta from Ukraine and Karam Al Kafri from Syria. Through poi
 gnant personal accounts\, filmed footage\, and live drawing\, they share
  their intimate stories and experiences\, shaped by war and exile. They 
 offer us a powerful perspective on resilience and commitment. In today
 ’s world\, where conflicts and migration are reshaping our reality\, V
 ivantes speaks to the importance of intergenerational transmission in pa
 ving the way for better times. “Radio live is a Noah’s Ark. The powe
 rful echo of the world through its inhabitants.” – Marie Plantin\nAu
 dience: Open to the Public\nhttps://www.princeton.edu/events/2026/seuls-
 en-scene-2026-presents-vivantes-aurelie-charon
URL:https://www.princeton.edu/events/2026/seuls-en-scene-2026-presents-vi
 vantes-aurelie-charon
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
END:VCALENDAR
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:6e35bd6ca6e63db56af3e9f8ca396bf5
DTSTAMP:20260920T025617Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260926
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260927
SUMMARY:Out After Dark: Exhibition by Tamara Santibañez
LOCATION:Hurley Gallery\, Lewis Arts Complex
DESCRIPTION:Out After Dark is an exhibition by 2025-2027 Princeton Arts F
 ellow Tamara Santibañez that calls attention to the bodily negotiations
  we make to access space within shifting landscapes of law and power\, s
 peculating on how we might rethink and remake these tacit agreements. He
 re\, the body is referenced less through its visuality than by its prese
 nce moving through the exhibit\, or the materiality of its encounter wit
 h the built environment. On display are the quotidian materials that mak
 e up the borders of the constructed world\, refabricated using alternati
 ve substances. Santibañez plays with the assumed durability and duratio
 n of various building sites and objects\, and alters their utility. Seem
 ingly indestructible materials are rendered vulnerable—the bricks foun
 d here are made of glass and paper\; the tough barrier of a leather jack
 et is made porous by a delicate glass etching lit up as i\nAudience: Ope
 n to the Public\nhttps://www.princeton.edu/events/2026/out-after-dark-ex
 hibition-tamara-santibanez-17
URL:https://www.princeton.edu/events/2026/out-after-dark-exhibition-tamar
 a-santibanez-17
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
END:VCALENDAR
